_noticia('cuando-trabajar-tu-marca', 'When is it time to work on your brand?',
         'When your business is born, when it grows, when you are compared on price, when your direction changes, or when people do not quite know what you do.',
         '<p>There is a question that comes up often when we talk about branding: do I really need to work on my brand now?</p><p>Sometimes the answer seems obvious. You are starting a business and need a name, an identity and a way to present yourself. Other times it is not so easy to recognize. The business has been running for years, you have clients, you sell products or services, but you feel that something no longer fits.</p><p>What you communicated at the beginning may no longer represent what you do today. Or your clients may not understand what sets you apart from other options.</p><p>In my experience, working on a brand is not something that happens only once. It is part of a business’s evolution. And there are moments when stopping to review it can open up interesting opportunities.</p><h2>When it is born</h2><p><strong>You have a good idea, but you still need to give it an identity.</strong></p><p>You are about to launch a business, a product or a new line of services. You are clear on what you want to offer, but you still need to find a way to present it to the world.</p><p>Here, brand work helps build a foundation. It is not only about finding a nice name or designing a logo. It is about understanding what you are offering, who you want to reach and how you want people to recognize you.</p><p>It is the moment to define a direction before you start communicating.</p><h2>When it grows</h2><p><strong>Your business has evolved, but your brand has stayed behind.</strong></p><p>You started by offering one service and now you have several. You added clients, brought people into the team or found new business opportunities.</p><p>It is a good problem to have, but it can also create some confusion. What used to be easy to explain now needs more context. Your brand no longer tells everything you do, or the image you project does not reflect the level your business has reached.</p><p>In these cases, working on the brand helps you organize what you have built, find a narrative that represents this new stage and bring coherence to your different services or products.</p><p>You do not necessarily have to start from scratch. Sometimes what matters is recognizing how much your business has changed and making your brand move in the same direction.</p><h2>When it competes</h2><p><strong>If everyone seems to offer the same thing, why should people choose you?</strong></p><p>There are markets where alternatives abound. Clients compare prices, features and benefits, and brands often end up communicating practically the same thing.</p><p>When that happens, competing only on price can become a race that is hard to sustain.</p><p>Working on your brand means finding what truly sets you apart and learning to communicate it. It may lie in the way you work, the experience you deliver, your knowledge or the relationships you build with your clients.</p><p>Differentiation does not mean inventing something extraordinary. It means recognizing what value you can bring and making it visible.</p><h2>When direction changes</h2><p><strong>The business you have today will not necessarily be the same tomorrow.</strong></p><p>Sometimes new opportunities appear: you want to enter another market, develop a product or look for funding to grow.</p><p>These changes also test your brand. Does it still represent what you want to build? Is the value proposition clear enough for the new people you need to reach?</p><p>At these moments, brand strategy can help you organize ideas, define a direction and build a narrative that supports the next step of the business.</p><p>It is not about changing for the sake of it, but about making sure your brand still makes sense in light of what you want to achieve.</p><h2>When people do not recognize you</h2><p><strong>You do far more than people can see.</strong></p><p>There are organizations and businesses with an important track record, specialized knowledge or a huge contribution to their community, but they struggle to communicate it.</p><p>Sometimes people do not quite understand what they do. Other times they know the organization but are unaware of everything behind it.</p><p>Here the challenge is not always having more presence. It is also finding a way to explain who you are, what you do and why it matters.</p><p>A good communication strategy helps organize messages, identify who you need to reach and find a clearer way to connect with each audience.</p><h2>A brand also needs to move forward</h2><p>There is no single moment to work on your brand. It can be when you are starting and need to find a direction; during growth, when you need to organize what you have built; or when something changes and you need to reconnect with people.</p><p>Nor does it always mean designing a new logo. Sometimes you need to review your strategy, other times improve how you communicate and, occasionally, completely rethink your identity.</p><p>What matters is understanding what is happening in your business before deciding what you need to do.</p><p><strong>Where is your brand right now?</strong></p><p>Tell me what you have in hand and let’s talk about how we can take it where you want to go.</p>',
         'Brand', _EN)

_noticia('un-buen-equipo-no-basta', 'A good tech team is not enough if no one sets the course',
         'These are signs that repeat in digital products: the client asks for one thing, the team delivers another, and no one is clear on what to build first.',
         '<p>Does any of this sound familiar?</p>' + _lista([
             'The client asks for one thing and the team delivers another.',
             'Requirements arrive vague and development drags on.',
             'No one is clear on what to build first, or why.',
             'Many AI ideas, but none reaches production.',
             'The product works, but it has no identity and people do not get it at first sight.']) +
         '<p>My job is to be the bridge between your business, your client and your tech team.</p>',
         'Digital product', _EN)

_noticia('tres-roles-de-producto', 'Design goes beyond design: business, brands and digital products',
         'In all those cases, design can offer much more than a visual solution.',
         '<p>There are projects that start with a good idea, but do not quite know how to make it real. Others have a technical team capable of building almost anything, but need to define what is worth building. And there are those with a good product who cannot communicate why someone should choose it.</p><p>In all those cases, design can offer much more than a visual solution.</p><p>Over time, my work as a designer has led me to get involved in different stages of a project: from understanding the business and defining a brand strategy, to organizing the development of a digital product and designing the experience of the people who use it.</p><p>That combination lets me approach projects from a broader perspective, connecting ideas with the people who need them and with the teams that must make them real.</p><h2>Understand the problem before looking for the solution</h2><p>What a business needs is not always a new website, an app or a visual identity. Sometimes what is missing is clarity: understanding which problem needs to be solved, what the priorities are and which path makes the most sense to follow.</p><p>That is why, before thinking about solutions, I care about understanding the context. What the business is looking for, what its users need and what conditions exist to bring an idea into practice.</p><p>This view is also part of my work as a Product Owner. In that role, I connect the needs of the business with the technical team, turning ideas and goals into concrete tasks that can be developed, tested and improved.</p><p>I work with user stories, a roadmap and a prioritized backlog, take part in Scrum ceremonies and review deliveries to check that they meet what was defined. I also help order expectations and keep the team focused on what really matters.</p><p>In short, I help everyone understand what we are building, why we are building it and what the next step is.</p><h2>Give meaning to what a brand wants to build</h2><p>Once we understand the business and its proposal, another question appears: how do we help people understand its value?</p><p>A brand is not just a logo or a color system. It is the way a company or a product presents itself, sets itself apart and builds a relationship with people. When that identity is not well defined, communicating a good idea can become much harder.</p><p>Through brand strategy, I work on finding that meaning and turning it into a clear direction. I define the purpose, the positioning and the elements that express what makes a product, or the company behind it, different.</p><p>This can include a Brand Compass to guide brand decisions, naming, visual identity, voice and tone of communication, and even the design of its website.</p><p>The goal is for every decision to have a reason behind it, and for the brand not only to look good but also to help communicate better what it offers.</p><h2>Turn ideas into experiences we can test</h2><p>With digital products, there is an important gap between having an idea and knowing whether it really works for the people who will use it.</p><p>UX/UI design helps close that gap. It lets me explore how a product should work, which steps a person needs to follow, and how we can make the experience more intuitive, useful and consistent with the business goals.</p><p>I work on user flows, navigation structure, interface design and the prototypes that let us explore a solution before fully developing it.</p><p>I also use artificial intelligence tools to take some of these ideas to navigable or functional prototypes more quickly. That way we can see the proposal more clearly, test alternatives and spot opportunities for improvement before committing more resources to development.</p><p>Once we have a clear direction, I prepare the design and the handover to the technical team so the solution can be implemented in production.</p><h2>A view that connects the different parts of a project</h2><p>My work is not limited to a single stage or a single discipline. I care about how the business, the brand, the user experience and the technology relate, because the decisions we make in one area end up influencing the others.</p><p>That perspective lets me collaborate with different profiles, talk with both business people and technical teams, and help design decisions respond to concrete needs.</p><p>It is not about doing everything, but about understanding what each project needs and contributing from where I can add the most value: giving direction to ideas, turning them into solutions and helping them reach the people they were made for.</p><p>In the end, designing also means connecting the pieces so things work better.</p>',
         'Services', _EN)

_noticia('metodo-scrum', 'Scrum, grounded in the business',
         """A team can be working at full speed and still not be moving toward what the business really needs. That is why, for me, working with agile methodologies starts well before organizing a sprint.""",
         """<p>When we talk about Scrum, it is common to think of meetings, boards, tasks and weekly deliveries. But after working coordinating development teams and managing digital products, I have learned that none of those things help much if we are not clear about what we are trying to solve.</p><p>You can have a very good team, build features and meet deadlines, but are we building what the business really needs?</p><p>That is where I find the value of working with agile methodologies. It is not about following a meeting calendar or filling a task board. It is about keeping the team connected to an objective, making decisions on time and learning from what we build.</p><p>This is how I approach project management using Scrum.</p><h2>01. Understand the business</h2><p><strong>Before thinking about features, we need to understand the problem.</strong></p><p>Every project starts with a need, but it does not always arrive well defined. Sometimes the client has a fairly clear idea of what they want to build, but we still need to understand why they need it and what they expect to achieve.</p><p>That is why my first step is to talk with the client, the stakeholders and, when appropriate, get close to the users. I need to understand how the business works, where its difficulties are and which opportunities we want to address.</p><p>This lets us avoid something quite common: starting to develop a solution without having truly understood the problem.</p><h2>02. Define what we are going to build</h2><p><strong>Ideas have to become decisions the team can carry out.</strong></p><p>Once we understand the problem, we need to translate it into something concrete. This is where the functional definition work begins: organizing the needs, identifying features, writing user stories and building a roadmap that lets us see where we are heading.</p><p>Not everything can be built at once. That is why, together with the team and the stakeholders, we work on prioritizing what makes the most sense to tackle first, considering the value for the business, the needs of the users and the technical possibilities.</p><p class="destacado">A good backlog is not an endless list of things to do. It is a tool for making decisions.</p><h2>03. Build in sprints</h2><p><strong>Working in short cycles lets us stay focused without losing flexibility.</strong></p><p>With the priorities defined, we organize the work into sprints. The team takes a set of user stories and works to turn them into features we can review.</p><p>My responsibility as Product Owner is to keep that direction. I coordinate the work, help solve blockers, clarify requirements and keep the stakeholders informed about progress and the decisions that come up along the way.</p><p>Things do not always turn out exactly as we planned. Technical difficulties, new needs or changes in priorities can appear. What matters is that we can address them without losing sight of the product’s objective.</p><h2>04. Review what we are building</h2><p><strong>A feature being developed does not necessarily mean it is finished.</strong></p><p>An important part of my work is reviewing that each user story meets what we defined. For that I take part in the QA process, checking the deliverables against the requirements and the expected criteria.</p><p>This lets us detect differences between what was requested and what was actually built, fix problems and keep small errors from piling up as the project advances.</p><p>It is common for a developer to say that something is almost ready, or technically ready. But “ready” means it works and that all of us have reviewed it, not only the person who built it. That detail that does not meet what was expected usually shows up only in the review, and avoiding it takes judgment: the experience to tell apart what looks finished from what really is.</p><p>I prefer to stop and review a feature before calling it finished rather than discovering later that we built something different from what we needed.</p><h2>05. Learn and adjust</h2><p><strong>A plan gives us direction, but it does not have to be set in stone.</strong></p><p>At the end of each cycle we review what was built, talk about progress and assess what we need to adjust.</p><p>Sometimes we find that a feature needs more work. Other times we understand that a priority has changed or that there is a better way to solve something.</p><p>The good thing about working iteratively is exactly that: we do not need to wait until the end of the project to find out whether we are on the right track.</p><p>Each delivery lets us learn something and use that learning to make better decisions in the next cycle.</p><h2>What I took away</h2><p>Over time I learned to recognize some warning signs that can affect a project long before they become a problem.</p><p>Unclear priorities, decisions without defined owners or expectations that were never agreed. They seem small at first, but they can end up generating rework, delays and frustration between teams.</p><p>Today I try to address these situations from day one. I prefer to ask an uncomfortable question at the beginning than to have to explain a problem at the end.</p><p>For me, working with Scrum does not simply mean organizing tasks better. It means building a way of working where the business, the team and technology can move in the same direction.</p><p>Because in the end, it is not about how many features we manage to deliver in a sprint, but about how close we get to solving the problem we set out to address.</p><p class="destacado">Building well also means knowing what is worth building.</p>""",
         'Method', _EN)

_noticia('ley-21719-prepara-tu-web', 'Law 21.719 prepares your website for fines',
         """Law 21.719 takes effect on December 1, 2026, although a bill to postpone it until 2027 has been introduced. Review forms, cookies and privacy notices on your website before that date.""",
         """<p>Your site could be collecting personal data without you noticing. Forms, cookies, analytics tools and external providers: find out what you should review before you expose yourself to legal problems.</p><p>Does your website collect names, emails or phone numbers from your clients? Do you use Google Analytics, contact forms or advertising tools? Do you know where that information is stored and who can access it?</p><p>If you do not have clear answers to these questions, you may have a problem you have not yet detected.</p><p>Law 21.719 changes the rules on how organizations collect, use and protect personal data in Chile. Although it is often discussed in relation to large companies and digital platforms, it can also affect small businesses, online stores and websites that receive information from their visitors.</p><p class="destacado">The problem is that many pages are built with selling, attracting clients or generating traffic in mind, but we rarely stop to review what happens to the information people leave on them.</p><p class="destacado">And that is precisely what you should start looking at.</p><h2>What changes with Law 21.719?</h2><p class="destacado">Law 21.719 modernizes the Chilean regulation on personal data protection, amends Law 19.628 and creates the Personal Data Protection Agency.</p><p>Among other things, it sets new requirements for processing personal information, strengthens people’s rights and establishes a regime of infractions and sanctions.</p><p>The law was published in December 2024 and its entry into force is scheduled for December 1, 2026. A bill submitted to the Senate in September 2026 proposes postponing it until 2027; until it is approved, the effective date remains 2026. Check the status of Bulletin 18.623-07 in the Senate and in the National Congress Library (bcn.cl).</p><p>This means that a generic privacy policy or a cookie notice is no longer enough. Organizations must be able to explain what information they collect, what they use it for, how they protect it and under what conditions they share it.</p><p>And what does this have to do with your website? Practically everything that happens when someone interacts with it can involve personal data.</p><h2>When could your website be at risk?</h2><p>You do not need a complex platform to run into these problems. A few fairly common features are enough.</p><h2>01. Contact forms</h2><p><strong>Are you asking for more information than you really need?</strong></p><p>Name, email, phone and company are data that many pages request without clearly explaining what they will be used for.</p><p>Review what information you are collecting, what its purpose is and how you inform people about its processing. You should also consider how you handle requests from people who want to exercise their rights.</p><h2>02. Cookies and tracking tools</h2><p><strong>Do you know what is happening while people browse your site?</strong></p><p>Google Analytics, Meta Pixel and other tools can collect information about your visitors’ behavior. They may also involve external services that receive or process data.</p><p>It is not simply a matter of installing a banner that says “accept cookies”. You need to identify which tools you use, what information they collect and how you manage consent when it applies.</p><h2>03. Privacy policy</h2><p><strong>Does your site really explain what you do with your users’ information?</strong></p><p>Many websites have privacy policies copied from other pages, outdated documents or texts that are hard to understand.</p><p>Your privacy notice must clearly explain what data you collect, for what purpose, how you use it, who may receive it and how people can exercise their rights.</p><p>Having the link in the footer is not enough. The information must be accessible and understandable.</p><h2>04. User rights</h2><p><strong>Do you know what to do when someone asks you to delete their data?</strong></p><p>The new regulation strengthens rights such as access, rectification, erasure, objection and portability of personal data.</p><p>This means you must have a procedure to receive, manage and respond to these requests.</p><p class="destacado">If someone asks you what information you hold about them or requests its deletion, you need to know who will take care of it and how to respond.</p><h2>05. Providers and external services</h2><p><strong>Who else has access to the data your website collects?</strong></p><p>Your hosting, e-commerce platform, email services, marketing tools and analytics systems may take part in processing the information.</p><p>It is important to identify which providers you use, what data you share with them and what responsibilities correspond to each one.</p><p>Sometimes the problem is not directly on your page, but in the tools you connected to make it work.</p><h2>Where should you start preparing your website?</h2><p>You do not need to rebuild your site from scratch or implement all the changes at once. The first step is to understand what is happening.</p><p>I would start with a diagnosis that identifies where your website collects, stores or shares personal information. This would be my initial list:</p><ul><li><strong>Data inventory:</strong> identify what information you request and where it is stored.</li><li><strong>Forms:</strong> review the fields, the purposes and the mechanisms for information and consent.</li><li><strong>Cookies and analytics:</strong> identify the tools installed and how you manage consent.</li><li><strong>Legal texts:</strong> review the privacy policies and the information available to users.</li><li><strong>Providers:</strong> identify which external services take part in the processing of data.</li></ul><p>With that information you can recognize the gaps, set priorities and plan the necessary improvements.</p><p>That said, a technical review does not replace a legal assessment. To determine your business’s specific obligations, it is important to get specialized advice.</p><p class="destacado">Before a problem turns into a fine, it is better to know where you stand.</p><p>Do not wait until you have a problem to worry about your website.</p><p class="destacado">Your website does not only represent your business: it is also a space where people trust information about themselves with every click.</p><p>Designing a good digital experience means thinking about more than attractive interfaces or forms that convert. It also means building experiences that are transparent, responsible and respectful of the people who use them.</p><p>If you have a website and do not know where to start, let’s talk. We can review how it works, identify opportunities for improvement and define what you need to resolve.</p><p><a class="link-arrow" href="{p}en/index.html#contacto">Talk to me to review it</a></p><p class="muted">This article provides general information and does not constitute legal advice. A technical review of a website does not, by itself, guarantee compliance with Law 21.719.</p><p class="muted">Last updated: October 30, 2026.</p>""",
         'Regulation', _EN)
